{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>Ben porat.</b> Of the language of branches, like ״מסעף פארה במערצה.״ Yosef is a son whose branch grew עלי עין and was planted על פלגי מים." "<b>עלי עין.</b> Such is the way of poetic speech, to double the expression, like ״נשאו נהרות ה׳ נשאו נהרות קולם,״ ״אנכי לה׳ אנכי אשירה,״ and many more." "<b>Daughters.</b> These are the branches that go out from the body of the tree, and it says that they are large and tall, up above the wall — this is צעדה עלי שור." "<b>צעדה</b> is a feminine plural form, like צעדו for males, for example ״וחטאתינו ענתה בנו,״ ״ויהי ידיו אמונה,״ ״והפשתה והשעורה נוכתה,״ ״בעצמותי וירדנה.״ And the metaphor is about Yosef, who was fruitful and multiplied from Efrayim and Menasheh, who were appointed as tribes. And also the sons of Yosef said to Yehoshua, ״ואני עם רב אשר עד כה ברכני ה׳.״ [בנות צעדה עלי שור — when Yosef passed through Egypt, all the women went out to see him on account of his beauty.]"], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {3 "The bracketed final sentence appears to be a secondary gloss preserved in the manuscript tradition; it is translated as found."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:09:19.682336812Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}